WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:05.791
Hopes, rumors, and then actual news of a movie
based on the long-running TV classic The Simpsons

00:00:05.791 --> 00:00:10.290
floated around for years before The Simpsons
Movie was released in 2007.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:11.599
It was worth the wait.

00:00:11.599 --> 00:00:15.679
A hilarious animated adventure with a big
story befitting the big screen, the film made

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:19.289
more than $500 million at the worldwide box
office.

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:23.380
It's been 10 years since everyone's favorite
four-fingered Springfield residents proved

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:27.050
they could make the jump from television—so
how come they haven't made The Simpsons Movie

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.050
2 yet?

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.050
A good idea

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:33.360
The show's writers and producers don't want
to attempt another big-screen project just

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.650
for the sake of doing it.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:38.620
It's such a monumental project, with millions
of dollars at stake and millions of fans with

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.480
exacting standards, that they'll only make
another Simpsons movie if they've got a really

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.800
good idea—one that's really worth a 90-minute
treatment.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:51.020
During a Simpsons panel at the 2016 San Diego
Comic-Con, writer-producer Al Jean addressed

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.430
the possibility of doing another Simpsons
movie.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:59.260
"We talk about it, but if we do it, we only
want to do it if it's going to be really good."

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:00.260
Already used it

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.580
At one point, the writers actually thought
they had a great idea for the next Simpsons

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:05.580
Movie.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.750
In an episode initially conceived as the 24th
season finale, Kang and Kodos, the humongous,

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:14.040
booming-voiced aliens that have appeared in
numerous "Treehouse of Horror" Halloween specials,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.920
kidnap the Simpson family from "Diz-Nee-Land"
and take them to their distant home plant

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:20.690
of Rigel 7, where Homer is nearly eaten.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:25.020
Al Jean, who co-wrote the episode with David
Mirkin, decided with Simpsons executive producer

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.350
James L. Brooks that the plot was so ambitious
that they should use it for the next Simpsons

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:30.350
Movie.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:31.350
Jean told Entertainment Weekly,

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:34.370
"...we were worried that people might think
it's an idea that's not canonical..."

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:38.350
—because the plot is so far gone from the
generally realistic scenarios of The Simpsons.

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:42.260
The script went back into the episode pile
and aired as "The Man Who Came to Be Dinner"

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:43.800
in 2015.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.010
Too many ideas

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:49.750
Perhaps the real problem isn't that the Simpsons
writers don't have a good idea for a sequel…

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:51.450
it's that they can't pick just one.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.310
For example, before they settled on the plot
of the first Simpsons Movie, the writers floated

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.049
several large-scale, movie-worthy concepts.

00:01:58.049 --> 00:02:02.300
The fourth season episode "Kamp Krusty," in
which Bart and Lisa attend a terrifying, decrepit

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.980
summer camp that descends into nightmarish
chaos, was nearly a movie.

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:10.590
Matt Groening wanted to make a Fantasia parody
called Simptasia, consisting of short animated

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:11.910
sequences set to music.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:16.390
And before his death in 1998, Phil Hartman
planned on making a live-action film about

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:19.080
his Simpsons character, B-movie actor Troy
McClure.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.790
"He can talk, he can talk, he can talk, he
can talk!"

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:23.790
“I can sing!”

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.420
Matt Groening isn't thrilled

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.319
Any Simpsons movie has to get the sign-off
from Simpsons creator and executive producer

00:02:29.319 --> 00:02:30.319
Matt Groening.

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:34.319
Even 30 years after his characters debuted
on The Tracey Ullman Show, he's still very

00:02:34.319 --> 00:02:38.540
much involved with any and all major decisions
affecting his prized creation.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.270
And as far as a sequel goes, he's in no rush.

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:42.270
Why?

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:43.270
The first one was a grind.

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:48.130
In a 2009 interview, Groening said the first
movie was an experience that left him "frustrated,"

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:51.849
in part because he and other members of the
creative team thought it would take two years

00:02:51.849 --> 00:02:54.160
to produce but it ended up taking four.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.920
While speaking at UCLA Law School in 2013,
Groening said that the first film, quote,

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.540
"stole animators from the show and drained
resources."

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:05.550
At the same speaking engagement, Simpsons
producer and director David Silverman estimated

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:09.450
that another movie would happen in "another
10, 15 years."

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:10.650
Busy Simpsons

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:15.920
James L. Brooks, a Simpsons executive producer
since the show debuted in 1989, told The Hollywood

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.580
Reporter that the Simpsons creative team hasn't
made another movie because they're "doing

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:20.760
a lot of other stuff."

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.700
Brooks meant that whatever time The Simpsons
makers have for work outside the show itself

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:29.520
is spent on Simpsons-related extracurricular
activities, such as a series of live Simpsons

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.379
shows at the Hollywood Bowl or developing
the scores of Simpsons bonus material for

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:34.550
the Simpsons World app.

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:37.400
"It's the all new Simpsons ride.”

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.459
“Happy times.”

00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:41.800
The Simpsons is still on TV

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:45.750
Probably the main impediment preventing The
Simpsons' writers, cast, and crew from making

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.340
another Simpsons movie is The Simpsons.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:52.440
The same people who'd make another movie are
already pretty busy making 22 episodes of

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:53.590
the show every year.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:58.019
That's a huge workload—episodes take months
between conception and full animation, so

00:03:58.019 --> 00:04:02.390
each is a lengthy and costly endeavor involving
a small army of employees.

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:07.030
Simpsons producer and director David Silverman
told Entertainment Weekly in 2017 that a sequel

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:08.030
is

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:10.770
"...daunting because it really knocked the
stuffing out of us to do the movie and the

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:12.110
show at the same time."

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:15.260
If the crew doesn't want to make a movie at
the same time as the show, don't expect that

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:16.650
sequel anytime soon.

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:20.199
The Simpsons has been renewed until at least
2019.

00:04:20.199 --> 00:04:22.029
Fox hasn't ordered one yet

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:26.210
While another Simpsons Movie would be a surefire
hit and a no-brainer for all involved, the

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:30.819
fact remains that studio Twentieth Century
Fox hasn't technically asked for one yet.

00:04:30.819 --> 00:04:34.300
The creative team isn't about to move mountains
to make another movie until they're officially

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.039
asked—and contracts are signed.

00:04:36.039 --> 00:04:40.330
Otherwise, why work on something that might
not get made anytime soon—or ever?

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:44.419
Nevertheless, Simpsons writer-producer Al
Jean says a movie is in its "earliest stages,"

00:04:44.419 --> 00:04:48.249
which probably means the show's writers are
tossing around ideas and getting a game plan

00:04:48.249 --> 00:04:52.889
together for if and when their corporate bosses
ever commission a Simpsons Movie sequel.

00:04:52.889 --> 00:04:55.159
Keep those stubby yellow fingers crossed!

00:04:55.159 --> 00:04:56.159
Thanks for watching!

